<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Karafuto Prefecture</h1><p class="st-meta">1905 CE – 1945 CE · capital Toyohara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Japan's colony on the southern half of Sakhalin island, won from Russia in 1905 and lost to the Soviet Union in 1945.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-portsmouth">Treaty of Portsmouth</a> that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> in 1905 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a> south of the 50th parallel. Japan set up the Karafuto Agency in 1907 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Otomari</a>, moving its seat in 1908 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuzhno-sakhalinsk">Toyohara</a>, the former Russian settlement of Vladimirovka.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Karafuto was run by a governor under the home government. Japanese settlers came to fish herring and salmon, cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and work in pulp and paper mills and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines, and the population reached about 400,000 by the 1940s, including Korean labourers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nivkh-people">Nivkh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orok-people">Uilta</a> communities were pressed to assimilate. In 1943 Karafuto was made part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> proper.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Soviet forces invaded in August 1945 and occupied the territory, and the Japanese population was repatriated by 1949, though many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> were left behind. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> renounced its claim in 1951, and the area is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin-oblast">Sakhalin Oblast</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karafuto-prefecture.jpg" alt="Karafuto Prefecture" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Kinchaku · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Karafuto.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1905 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Toyohara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shinto, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karafuto, Japanese Sakhalin, South Sakhalin</dd></dl></aside></div>

